mbar - minimal status bar for mango
===================================
Tags on the left, dwmblocks-style blocks on the right. Docks to the
top edge via layer-shell. Gruvbox dark.

Single C file. Depends on libwayland-client only. Text is anti-aliased
TTF rendered by the bundled public-domain stb_truetype - no gtk, no
cairo, no pango, no freetype.

Requirements
------------
wayland (libwayland-client + wayland-scanner), a C compiler, make.
Protocol XMLs are vendored in protocols/.

Installation
------------
    make
    sudo make install

Run mbar from your mango autostart.

Configuration
-------------
mbar is configured by editing the config block at the top of bar.c
and recompiling: colors ("#rrggbb" or "#aarrggbb"), bar height, font
size, separator, padding.

Fonts: the first existing path in fontpaths[] wins. Override without
recompiling:

    MBAR_FONT=/path/to/font.ttf mbar

Tags: current = block, urgent = red, hidden = gray. Event-driven via
ext-workspace-v1.

Blocks
------
A block is a function that writes a string. Add it to blocks[]:

    static void blk_bat(char *o, size_t n) {
            FILE *f = fopen("/sys/class/power_supply/BAT0/capacity", "r");
            int c = 0; if (f) { fscanf(f, "%d", &c); fclose(f); }
            snprintf(o, n, "BAT %d%%", c);
    }
    static void (*blocks[])(char *, size_t) = { blk_bat, blk_date, blk_time };

For scripts, use blk_cmd with a per-block cache and interval:

    static void blk_vol(char *o, size_t n) {
            static struct cmdcache c;
            blk_cmd("volume.sh", 2, &c, o, n);
    }

Everything redraws once per second; blk_cmd runs its command every
N seconds and serves the cached line in between.
